**Reversing a String in Java**
================================

Here's a simple function that takes a string as input and returns the reversed string.

### Using StringBuilder

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(reverseString("Hello World"));
    }

    public static String reverseString(String str) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(str);
        return sb.reverse().toString();
    }
}
```

In this code, we're using the `StringBuilder` class, which has a `reverse()` method that reverses the string.

### Using a Loop

If you don't want to use `StringBuilder`, you can use a simple loop to reverse the string.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(reverseString("Hello World"));
    }

    public static String reverseString(String str) {
        char[] arr = str.toCharArray();
        int left = 0;
        int right = arr.length - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            char temp = arr[left];
            arr[left] = arr[right];
            arr[right] = temp;
            left++;
            right--;
        }

        return new String(arr);
    }
}
```

In this code, we're converting the string to a character array and then swapping the characters from the start and end of the array, moving towards the center.

### Using Recursion

Here's a recursive function to reverse a string.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(reverseString("Hello World"));
    }

    public static String reverseString(String str) {
        if (str.isEmpty()) {
            return str;
        } else {
            return reverseString(str.substring(1)) + str.charAt(0);
        }
    }
}
```

In this code, we're using the `substring()` method to remove the first character of the string and then recursively calling the function with the remaining string. We're appending the first character to the end of the reversed string.

Note: Recursive solutions can be less efficient than iterative solutions, especially for large strings.

